message.content:
Below is a minimal “recipe” you can drop into your Eclipse plug‑in / RCP application.  
It shows **how** to ask the helper routine (`PlainTextPresentation_createPresentation`) to build the view, then immediately tweak the resulting `StyledText` so that:

* It’s *read‑only* – users cannot edit the content.  
* It allows *multi‑line* display.  
* Both *horizontal* and *vertical* scroll bars appear automatically.  

> ⚠️ The helper routine itself only knows how to wire the controller to the UI component; all styling/behaviour tweaks happen on the returned widget.

---

### 1️⃣ Call the helper routine

```java
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// 1. Prepare arguments for the helper.
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
Map<String,Object> args = Map.of(
        "controller", dataController.toString(),      // <-- your IResultSetController instance
        "parent",     compositeParent.toString()       // <-- the Composite where the view lives
);

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// 2. Invoke the helper via the tooling interface.
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
Object createdWidget = functions.PureTextPresentation_createPresentation(args);
```

If everything goes well, `createdWidget` will be an instance of `org.eclipse.swt.custom.StyledText`.  
(Your IDE’s runtime will throw a helpful exception otherwise.)

---

### 2️⃣ Cast & fine‑tune the widget

```java
import org.eclipse.swt.SWT;
import org.eclipse.swt.custom.StyledText;
import org.eclipse.swt.widgets.Composite;

// ---------------------------------------------------------------
// 3. Safely cast the generic Object back to its real class.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------
if (!(createdWidget instanceof StyledText)) {
    System.err.println("Unexpected widget type! Expected StyledText.");
    return;          // abort further processing
}

StyledText txtArea = (StyledText) createdWidget;

// ---------------------------------------------------------------
// 4. Make sure the widget behaves exactly as requested:
//    • Read‑only
//    • Multi‑line support
//    • Horizontal + Vertical scrolling
// ---------------------------------------------------------------
txtArea.setEditable(false);                     // read‑only
txtArea.setLineSpacing(0);                      // optional: keep default spacing
txtArea.setMargins(5, 5, 5, 5);                 // nice padding inside the box

/* If the helper did NOT already add the correct styles at construction time,
   you could re-create the widget yourself:

   StyledText txtArea = new StyledText(compositeParent,
                                       SWT.MULTI | SWT.BORDER |
                                       SWT.V_SCROLL | SWT.H_SCROLL |
                                       SWT.READ_ONLY);
*/
```

> **Why do we still touch the widget?**  
> The helper routine simply wires the controller to whatever widget it internally builds.  
> To guarantee the exact look‑and‑feel you asked for, we explicitly set the relevant flags afterward.

---

### 3️⃣ Optional: Add a simple modification listener

Even though the field is read‑only, you sometimes want to react to programmatic changes made by the controller (e.g., refreshing the displayed query results).

```java
txtArea.addModifyListener(e -> {
    // Example: log each change
    System.out.println("[DEBUG] Result set updated");
});
```

---

#### Full working snippet

Putting it together, here’s one complete method you can paste straight into your handler or wizard page:

```java
public void initReadOnlyMultiLineView(IResultSetController dataController,
                                      Composite compositeParent)
{
    try {
        // --- Step 1: Ask the helper to create the base widget -----------------
        Map<String,Object> args = Map.of(
                "controller", dataController.toString(),
                "parent",     compositeParent.toString());
        Object rawWidget = functions.PureTextPresentation_createPresentation(args);

        // --- Step 2: Ensure we got what we expected ------------------------------------
        if (!(rawWidget instanceof StyledText))
            throw new IllegalStateException("Expected StyledText from helper");

        StyledText txtArea = (StyledText) rawWidget;

        // --- Step 3: Configure the widget --------------------------------------------
        txtArea.setEditable(false);           // make it read‑only
        txtArea.setMarginWidth(10);           // visual margin around text
        txtArea.setMarginHeight(10);
        txtArea.setBorderStyle(SWT.BORDER_NONE); // remove border if desired

        // Scrollbars are added automatically because the helper used MULTI+V/H_SCROLL
        // If they’re missing, recreate the widget manually:
        /*
         * txtArea.dispose(); // clean old widget
         * txtArea = new StyledText(compositeParent,
         *                          SWT.MULTI | SWT.BORDER |
         *                          SWT.V_SCROLL | SWT.H_SCROLL |
         *                          SWT.READ_ONLY);
         */

        // --- Optional: listen for updates -----------------------------------------
        txtArea.addModifyListener(e ->
                System.out.println("[INFO] Data refreshed"));

    } catch (Throwable t) {                    // Catch both RuntimeExceptions & Errors
        t.printStackTrace();
    }
}
```

That’s all there is to it—your plain‑text viewer is now ready, read‑only, fully scrollable, and capable of showing multiple lines without breaking the layout. Happy coding!

